//? Title: Activation Functions: Shapes, Gaussian Moments and Depth
//? Subtitle: Seventeen elementwise nonlinearities and the gated GLU family, checked against their closed forms; how an activation's Gaussian moments fix its weight scale and decide whether signals and gradients survive depth; SELU's constants derived from two moment equations; and a deep network on digits, using datamunge's nn::activate, nn::glu and nn::activation_moments
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An activation function is a scalar nonlinearity applied elementwise between linear layers. Its shape matters less than two numbers: under a Gaussian input
\(Z\sim\mathcal N(0,q)\), the second moments \(\mathbb E[f(Z)^2]\) and \(\mathbb E[f'(Z)^2]\). The first fixes the weight scale that keeps the forward signal's variance constant. The second
then decides whether gradients grow or shrink per layer. This report checks 17 activations and four gated linear units against their closed forms, tabulates both moments for
every activation, and derives SELU's constants \(\lambda\) and \(\alpha\) in closed form from the two equations \(\mathbb E[f]=0\) and \(\mathbb E[f^2]=1\), reproducing the published values to 15 significant digits.
Mean-field predictions match 30-layer networks to within 6% for SELU, ELU and tanh, forwards and backwards. They also show that a plain stack of gated units has a *repelling* fixed point, which
explains why a deep SwiGLU network without residual connections never leaves chance on the digits data, while the same units in pre-norm residual blocks train at once. The library gained
four activations (`HardSigmoid`, `HardSwish`, `HardTanh`, `LogSigmoid`), `nn::glu` and `nn::activation_moments`.
)

\toc

> The problem

Without a nonlinearity between them, a stack of linear layers is one linear map. The activation \(f\) that breaks this is usually chosen by habit: ReLU for convolutional networks,
GELU or SwiGLU for transformers, tanh inside recurrent cells. The choice interacts with the initialisation, since a weight scale that suits one activation can make the signal vanish or explode under another,
and with the architecture, since some units only work inside residual blocks. This report asks what each activation looks like, which numbers decide how it behaves in a deep network, and
whether those numbers predict what happens in practice.

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.2_reusable_layer_library/activations.cpp`. That example checked 13 activations' gradients against closed forms on a
grid, listed their statistics on \(\mathcal N(0,1)\), and showed SELU keeping its scale through 30 layers. All three are kept here, extended to the four new activations and the gated units, and joined by the theory
that explains the third.

> The method

>> Four families

`nn::activate(x, kind, alpha)` applies one of the `nn::Activation` kinds elementwise, through ordinary differentiable ops, so autograd supplies \(f'\).

- *Rectifiers.* ReLU \(\max(x,0)\) (Nair and Hinton, 2010), leaky ReLU \(\max(x,ax)\) (Maas et al., 2013), ELU, which is \(x\) for \(x>0\) and \(e^x-1\) otherwise (Clevert et al., 2016), and SELU \(\lambda\,\mathrm{elu}_\alpha(x)\) (Klambauer et al., 2017).
- *Smooth rectifiers.* GELU \(x\Phi(x)\) and its tanh approximation (Hendrycks and Gimpel, 2016), SiLU or swish \(x\sigma(x)\) (Elfwing et al., 2018; Ramachandran et al., 2017), Mish \(x\tanh(\operatorname{softplus}x)\) (Misra, 2020) and softplus \(\log(1+e^x)\).
  The first three dip below zero for negative inputs and are not monotone.
- *Saturating.* Sigmoid \(\sigma(x)=1/(1+e^{-x})\), tanh, softsign \(x/(1+\lvert x\rvert)\) and log-sigmoid \(\log\sigma(x)=-\operatorname{softplus}(-x)\).
- *Piecewise-linear.* ReLU6 \(\min(\max(x,0),6)\), hard sigmoid \(\mathrm{relu6}(x+3)/6\), hard swish \(x\,\mathrm{relu6}(x+3)/6\) (Howard et al., 2019) and hard tanh \(\operatorname{clip}(x,-1,1)\), cheap stand-ins for their smooth namesakes.

At a kink the derivative is undefined. Autograd returns one one-sided value, and the report records which.

>> Gaussian moments decide the weight scale

The report on initialisers derived the mean-field recursion for a deep network with weights of variance \(s/n_{\text{in}}\). If layer \(l\)'s pre-activations are approximately \(\mathcal N(0,q_l)\), then

$$
q_{l+1}=s\,\mathbb E\big[f(\sqrt{q_l}\,Z)^2\big],\qquad
\frac{\operatorname{Var}\delta_l}{\operatorname{Var}\delta_{l+1}}=\chi_l=s\,\mathbb E\big[f'(\sqrt{q_l}\,Z)^2\big],
$$
\alttext(q l plus 1 equals s times the expected value of f of root q l times Z, squared. The ratio of the gradient variances at layers l and l plus 1 is chi l, which equals s times the expected value of f prime of root q l times Z, squared.)

where \(\delta_l\) is the gradient of the loss with respect to layer \(l\)'s pre-activations and \(Z\sim\mathcal N(0,1)\) (Poole et al., 2016; Schoenholz et al., 2017). The forward signal keeps \(q=1\) when
\(s=1/\mathbb E[f(Z)^2]\), the square of the activation's natural gain. At that scale the gradient's per-layer factor is

$$
\chi^\ast=\frac{\mathbb E[f'(Z)^2]}{\mathbb E[f(Z)^2]}.
$$
\alttext(chi star equals the expected value of f prime of Z squared divided by the expected value of f of Z squared.)

If \(\chi^\ast<1\), gradients vanish geometrically with depth (the ordered phase). If \(\chi^\ast>1\), they grow (the chaotic phase). For a positively homogeneous activation, \(f(x)=xf'(x)\) gives \(\chi^\ast=1\)
exactly: for ReLU, both moments equal \(1/2\). `nn::activation_moments(kind, q)` computes \(\mathbb E f\), \(\mathbb E f^2\), \(\operatorname{Var}f\), \(\mathbb E f'\) and \(\mathbb E f'^2\) under \(\mathcal N(0,q)\) by the midpoint rule
on about 24 000 cells over \(\pm12\sqrt q\), with \(f'\) from autograd. Every kink of the built-in activations sits at an integer (0, \(\pm1\), \(\pm3\), 6), and a jump in \(f'\) on a cell boundary costs the midpoint
rule nothing, so the cells are aligned to the integers.

>> SELU's constants from two equations

Klambauer et al. (2017) chose \(\lambda\) and \(\alpha\) so that \(\mathcal N(0,1)\) inputs give outputs with mean 0 and variance 1. Both moments have closed forms. For \(Z\sim\mathcal N(0,1)\),
\(\mathbb E[Z\,1\{Z>0\}]=\varphi(0)=1/\sqrt{2\pi}\) and \(\mathbb E[Z^2\,1\{Z>0\}]=1/2\). Completing the square in \(e^z\varphi(z)=e^{1/2}\varphi(z-1)\) gives \(\mathbb E[e^Z\,1\{Z<0\}]=\sqrt e\,\Phi(-1)\), and
likewise \(\mathbb E[e^{2Z}\,1\{Z<0\}]=e^2\,\Phi(-2)\). Hence

$$
m_1=\mathbb E\big[(e^Z-1)1\{Z<0\}\big]=\sqrt e\,\Phi(-1)-\tfrac12,\qquad
m_2=\mathbb E\big[(e^Z-1)^21\{Z<0\}\big]=e^2\Phi(-2)-2\sqrt e\,\Phi(-1)+\tfrac12 .
$$
\alttext(m 1, the expected value of e to the Z minus 1 on Z less than 0, equals root e times Phi of minus 1, minus one half. m 2, the expected value of e to the Z minus 1 squared on Z less than 0, equals e squared times Phi of minus 2, minus 2 root e times Phi of minus 1, plus one half.)

The mean equation \(\lambda(\varphi(0)+\alpha m_1)=0\) does not involve \(\lambda\), and the variance equation then fixes it:

$$
\alpha=-\frac{\varphi(0)}{m_1},\qquad \lambda=\Big(\tfrac12+\alpha^2m_2\Big)^{-1/2}.
$$
\alttext(alpha equals minus phi of 0 divided by m 1. lambda equals one half plus alpha squared times m 2, all to the power minus one half.)

>> Gated linear units

A gated linear unit splits its input \(x\) into halves \(a\) and \(b\) and returns \(a\odot g(b)\) (Dauphin et al., 2017). With \(g=\sigma\) it is GLU, and with \(g\) equal to SiLU, GELU or ReLU it is SwiGLU, GeGLU or ReGLU,
the feed-forward units of most recent transformers (Shazeer, 2020). `nn::glu(x, gate, axis)` implements all four. A gated unit is a *product* of two pre-activations. If \(a\) and \(b\) are
independent \(\mathcal N(0,q)\), then \(\mathbb E[(a\,g(b))^2]=q\,\mathbb E[g(\sqrt q\,Z)^2]\), and the mean-field map becomes \(q\mapsto s\,q\,\mathbb E[g(\sqrt q\,Z)^2]\). For SiLU this grows faster than linearly in \(q\), which matters below.

> Simple examples

>> Every activation against its closed form

The program below writes \(f\) and \(f'\) for every activation out by hand and compares them with `nn::activate` and its autograd gradient on 8001 points in \([-8,8]\), skipping the kinks for \(f'\).
It also prints the original example's grid, the derivatives autograd returns at the kinks and the minima of the non-monotone rectifiers. Finally it checks the four gated units by finite
differences, to first and second order.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/activations_curves.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Activation;
using nn::Tensor;

// Closed forms of f and f', written out independently of the library.
constexpr double kLambda = 1.0507009873554804934193349852946, kAlpha = 1.6732632423543772848170429916717, kLeak = 0.2;
double sig(double x) { return 1 / (1 + std::exp(-x)); }
double softplus(double x) { return x > 30 ? x : std::log1p(std::exp(x)); }
double pdf(double x) { return std::exp(-0.5 * x * x) / std::sqrt(2 * std::numbers::pi); }
double cdf(double x) { return 0.5 * std::erfc(-x / std::sqrt(2.0)); }
double F(Activation a, double x) {
  const double c = std::sqrt(2 / std::numbers::pi);
  switch (a) {
    case Activation::None: return x;
    case Activation::Relu: return std::max(x, 0.0);
    case Activation::Relu6: return std::clamp(x, 0.0, 6.0);
    case Activation::LeakyRelu: return x > 0 ? x : kLeak * x;
    case Activation::Elu: return x > 0 ? x : std::expm1(x);
    case Activation::Selu: return kLambda * (x > 0 ? x : kAlpha * std::expm1(x));
    case Activation::Gelu: return x * cdf(x);
    case Activation::GeluTanh: return 0.5 * x * (1 + std::tanh(c * (x + 0.044715 * x * x * x)));
    case Activation::Silu: return x * sig(x);
    case Activation::Mish: return x * std::tanh(softplus(x));
    case Activation::Softplus: return softplus(x);
    case Activation::Softsign: return x / (1 + std::abs(x));
    case Activation::Sigmoid: return sig(x);
    case Activation::Tanh: return std::tanh(x);
    case Activation::HardSigmoid: return std::clamp(x + 3, 0.0, 6.0) / 6;
    case Activation::HardSwish: return x * std::clamp(x + 3, 0.0, 6.0) / 6;
    case Activation::HardTanh: return std::clamp(x, -1.0, 1.0);
    case Activation::LogSigmoid: return -softplus(-x);
  }
  return x;
}
double dF(Activation a, double x) {
  const double c = std::sqrt(2 / std::numbers::pi);
  switch (a) {
    case Activation::None: return 1;
    case Activation::Relu: return x > 0;
    case Activation::Relu6: return x > 0 && x < 6;
    case Activation::LeakyRelu: return x > 0 ? 1 : kLeak;
    case Activation::Elu: return x > 0 ? 1 : std::exp(x);
    case Activation::Selu: return kLambda * (x >= 0 ? 1 : kAlpha * std::exp(x));
    case Activation::Gelu: return cdf(x) + x * pdf(x);
    case Activation::GeluTanh: {
      const double t = std::tanh(c * (x + 0.044715 * x * x * x));
      return 0.5 * (1 + t) + 0.5 * x * (1 - t * t) * c * (1 + 3 * 0.044715 * x * x);
    }
    case Activation::Silu: return sig(x) * (1 + x * (1 - sig(x)));
    case Activation::Mish: { const double t = std::tanh(softplus(x)); return t + x * (1 - t * t) * sig(x); }
    case Activation::Softplus: return sig(x);
    case Activation::Softsign: return 1 / ((1 + std::abs(x)) * (1 + std::abs(x)));
    case Activation::Sigmoid: return sig(x) * (1 - sig(x));
    case Activation::Tanh: return 1 - std::tanh(x) * std::tanh(x);
    case Activation::HardSigmoid: return x > -3 && x < 3 ? 1.0 / 6 : 0;
    case Activation::HardSwish: return x <= -3 ? 0 : x >= 3 ? 1 : (2 * x + 3) / 6;
    case Activation::HardTanh: return x > -1 && x < 1;
    case Activation::LogSigmoid: return sig(-x);
  }
  return 1;
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<Activation> all;
  for (int k = 1; k <= static_cast<int>(nn::kLastActivation); ++k) all.push_back(static_cast<Activation>(k));
  const std::vector<double> grid{-4, -2, -1, -0.5, 0, 0.5, 1, 2, 4};
  // Autograd against the closed forms on a fine grid of 8001 points over [-8, 8], away from the kinks.
  std::print("| activation |");
  for (double g : grid) std::print(" f({}) |", g);
  std::print(" largest \\|f − closed form\\| | largest \\|autograd f′ − closed form\\| |\n| :--- |");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < grid.size() + 2; ++i) std::print(" ---: |");
  std::print("\n");
  std::vector<double> fine;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 8000; ++i) fine.push_back(-8 + 0.002 * i);
  for (Activation a : all) {
    const auto gv = nn::activate(nn::from_vector(grid, {9}), a, kLeak).to_vector<double>();
    Tensor x = nn::from_vector(fine, {8001}).requires_grad_(true);
    const Tensor y = nn::activate(x, a, kLeak);
    const auto yv = y.detach().to_vector<double>(), dv = nn::grad({nn::sum(y)}, {x})[0].to_vector<double>();
    double ef = 0, ed = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < fine.size(); ++i) {
      const double xi = fine[i];
      ef = std::max(ef, std::abs(yv[i] - F(a, xi)));
      const bool kink = std::abs(xi) < 1e-9 || std::abs(xi - 6) < 1e-9 || std::abs(std::abs(xi) - 3) < 1e-9 || std::abs(std::abs(xi) - 1) < 1e-9;
      if (!kink) ed = std::max(ed, std::abs(dv[i] - dF(a, xi)));
    }
    std::print("| {} |", nn::activation_name(a));
    for (double v : gv) std::print(" {:.3f} |", v);
    std::print(" {:.1e} | {:.1e} |\n", ef, ed);
  }
  // At the kinks the library picks one side, as TensorFlow does.
  Tensor k = nn::tensor({0.0, -3.0, 3.0, 6.0, -1.0, 1.0}, {6}, nn::DType::Float64).requires_grad_(true);
  std::print("\nAutograd at kinks x = (0, -3, 3, 6, -1, 1): relu′ {}, relu6′ {}, hard_sigmoid′ {}, hard_swish′ {}, hard_tanh′ {}, selu′(0) = {:.4f}.\n",
             nn::grad({nn::sum(nn::activate(k, Activation::Relu))}, {k})[0].values_str(), nn::grad({nn::sum(nn::activate(k, Activation::Relu6))}, {k})[0].values_str(),
             nn::grad({nn::sum(nn::activate(k, Activation::HardSigmoid))}, {k})[0].values_str(4), nn::grad({nn::sum(nn::activate(k, Activation::HardSwish))}, {k})[0].values_str(4),
             nn::grad({nn::sum(nn::activate(k, Activation::HardTanh))}, {k})[0].values_str(), nn::grad({nn::sum(nn::activate(k, Activation::Selu))}, {k})[0].to_vector<double>()[0]);

  // The non-monotone smooth rectifiers have a minimum below zero: solve f'(x) = 0 by bisection on [-3, 0].
  for (Activation a : {Activation::Gelu, Activation::Silu, Activation::Mish, Activation::HardSwish}) {
    double lo = -3, hi = -0.01;
    for (int it = 0; it < 200; ++it) { const double mid = 0.5 * (lo + hi); (dF(a, mid) < 0 ? lo : hi) = mid; }
    std::print("{}: minimum f = {:.4f} at x = {:.4f}. ", nn::activation_name(a), F(a, 0.5 * (lo + hi)), 0.5 * (lo + hi));
  }
  std::print("\n");

  // GLU family: a * g(b) on the last axis, checked by finite differences, first and second derivatives.
  nn::Generator gen(3);
  const nn::Tensor u = nn::normal({5, 8}, 0.0, 1.0, gen, nn::DType::Float64).requires_grad_(true);
  for (auto [name, gate] : std::vector<std::pair<std::string, Activation>>{{"glu", Activation::Sigmoid}, {"swiglu", Activation::Silu}, {"geglu", Activation::Gelu}, {"reglu", Activation::Relu}}) {
    auto f = [gate](const std::vector<nn::Tensor>& in) { return nn::glu(in[0], gate); };
    const auto g1 = nn::gradcheck(f, {u}), g2 = nn::gradgradcheck(f, {u});
    std::print("{}: output shape {}, gradcheck max error {:.1e} ({}), gradgradcheck {}. ", name, nn::shape_str(nn::glu(u, gate).shape()), g1.max_abs_error,
               g1.ok ? "pass" : "FAIL", g2.ok ? "pass" : "FAIL");
  }
  std::print("\n");

  // Figure: four families of activations.
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::vector<Activation>>> groups = {
      {"Rectifiers", {Activation::Relu, Activation::LeakyRelu, Activation::Elu, Activation::Selu}},
      {"Smooth rectifiers", {Activation::Gelu, Activation::Silu, Activation::Mish, Activation::Softplus}},
      {"Saturating", {Activation::Sigmoid, Activation::Tanh, Activation::Softsign, Activation::LogSigmoid}},
      {"Piecewise-linear (hard)", {Activation::Relu6, Activation::HardSigmoid, Activation::HardSwish, Activation::HardTanh}}};
  const dm::graphics::RGB cols[] = {{37, 99, 235}, {234, 88, 12}, {22, 163, 74}, {220, 38, 38}};
  std::vector<double> xs;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 400; ++i) xs.push_back(-4 + 0.02 * i);
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2);
  for (const auto& [title, acts] : groups) {
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < acts.size(); ++j) {
      std::vector<double> ys;
      for (double x : xs) ys.push_back(F(acts[j], x));
      p.lines(xs, ys, nn::activation_name(acts[j]), cols[j], 2.0);
    }
    p.lines({-4.0, 4.0}, {0.0, 0.0}, "", dm::graphics::RGB{203, 213, 225}, 1.0);
    p.x_limits(-4, 4).y_limits(-4.5, title == "Saturating" ? 3.5 : 8.5).title(title).x_label("x").y_label("f(x)");
    fig.add(p);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 820).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/activations_curves.svg");
}
```
