// result_end

\caption(The RMS of pre-activations (left) and of the loss gradient with respect to them (right), on a log10 scale, for 30-layer networks of width 256 with LeCun-normal weights. Points: one network each. Lines: the mean-field prediction.)
\alttext(Left: SELU stays at 0 throughout; ELU and tanh fall together to about minus 0.6 at layer 10, then ELU continues to minus 1.04 and tanh to minus 0.87 at layer 30; ReLU falls on a straight line to about minus 4.4 at layer 30, its points slightly below the line at the end. Right, read from layer 30 back to layer 1: all four start near 0 at layer 30; SELU rises to about 0.48 at layer 1; ELU falls to about minus 1 and tanh to about minus 0.8; ReLU falls on a straight line to about minus 4.5. Points sit on their lines throughout.)

*The table.* The ReLU moments are exact: \(\mathbb E f=1/\sqrt{2\pi}=0.3989\), and \(\mathbb E f^2=\mathbb E f'=\mathbb E f'^2=1/2\). Hard sigmoid's \(\mathbb E f'=P(\lvert Z\rvert<3)/6=0.1662\) and SELU's \(\mathbb E f'^2=\lambda^2(\tfrac12+\alpha^2e^2\Phi(-2))=1.0716\) agree with
their closed forms. The natural gain \(1/\sqrt{\mathbb E f^2}\) reproduces PyTorch's ReLU gain \(\sqrt2\) and leaky-ReLU gain \(\sqrt{2/(1+a^2)}=1.3868\). For tanh it is 1.5925, a little below the conventional 5/3, which the
initialiser report found to be slightly chaotic. For SELU it is exactly 1, the LeCun scale that self-normalisation assumes. PyTorch's `calculate_gain` returns 3/4 for SELU, and its documentation recommends gain 1 for self-normalising networks.

The last column sorts the activations into phases. The positively homogeneous ones (ReLU, leaky ReLU, and ReLU6 near the origin) have \(\chi^\ast=1.0000\), as derived. ELU and the smooth rectifiers sit just
inside the chaotic phase, at 1.04 (ELU) to 1.08 (hard swish), and so does SELU at 1.0716. Tanh, softsign and hard tanh are further in, at 1.18 to 1.32. Sigmoid, hard sigmoid, softplus and log-sigmoid are deep in the
ordered phase, at 0.10 to 0.32. Their non-zero mean (0.5 for the sigmoids, \(\pm0.81\) for softplus and log-sigmoid) inflates \(\mathbb E f^2\) without adding slope, so at the gain that holds the forward signal,
gradients shrink by a factor of 3 to 10 in variance per layer.

*SELU.* The closed forms give \(\lambda=1.0507009873554805\), identical to the published constant, and \(\alpha=1.6732632423543778\), which differs from it in the 16th significant digit. Quadrature
confirms \(\mathbb E\,\mathrm{selu}(Z)=-1.2\times10^{-8}\) and \(\mathbb E\,\mathrm{selu}(Z)^2=1.00000000\). The fixed point attracts: the variance map moves \(q=0.25\) to 0.71 and \(q=4\) to 1.39 in six layers, slowly
but in the right direction. Yet \(\chi^\ast=1.0716\), so even at its fixed point SELU grows gradient variance by 7% per layer.

*Depth.* The mean field predicts all four networks well. At layer 30 the measured RMS is 0.999 for SELU (predicted 1), 0.0909 for ELU (0.0897) and 0.134 for tanh (0.133). At LeCun scale ReLU halves the variance at
each layer, predicting \(2^{-29/2}=4.3\times10^{-5}\) at layer 30. The measured value, \(3.2\times10^{-5}\), is within the finite-width fluctuation the initialiser report described. Backwards, SELU's layer-1 gradient is 3.0 times its layer-30
value (predicted \(\prod\chi^{1/2}=2.82\)). ELU and tanh gradients shrink to 0.101 and 0.161 (predicted 0.096 and 0.155), and ReLU's to \(2.8\times10^{-5}\) (\(3.0\times10^{-5}\)). SELU is the only one of the four whose forward signal is
stable at \(s=1\), as the original example showed. Its gradients, however, are not.

*The gated map.* At \(s=1/\mathbb E[\mathrm{silu}(Z)^2]\), \(q=1\) is a fixed point of the SwiGLU map, but its slope there is 2.17. For an ordinary activation the map \(q\mapsto s\,\mathbb E f(\sqrt q Z)^2\) has slope at most 1 at
its fixed point (exactly 1 for ReLU). The gated map has an extra factor of \(q\), so the fixed point repels. Starting from \(q=0.9\) or \(1.1\), nine layers carry the variance to \(2.5\times10^{-37}\) or past \(10^{16}\). Starting from
\(q=0.206\), the first-layer variance a centred digit image gives (below), it reaches \(10^{-26}\) by the fifth layer.

> A dataset: activations in a deep network on digits

The digits data (Alpaydin and Kaynak, 1998) give 1797 handwritten digits as \(8\times8\) images with grey levels 0 to 16. Scale the pixels to \([0,1]\), centre them, train on the first
400 and test on the last 360 (a small training set keeps the fourteen fits quick). Each network has ten bias-free hidden layers of width 64 and a linear output layer, initialised with each activation's natural scale
\(s=1/\mathbb E f(Z)^2\) from the table. Training is 60 full-batch Adam steps in single precision at learning rate 0.003, repeated for two seeds. SwiGLU layers map 64 to 128 and gate back down to 64. The two
"+ res" networks use pre-norm residual blocks, \(h\leftarrow h+f(h/\mathrm{RMS}(h)\,W)\), with LeCun weights in the branch, the arrangement of a transformer's feed-forward block (Xiong et al., 2020).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/activations_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
namespace init = dm::nn::init;
using nn::Activation;
using nn::Tensor;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto F32 = nn::DType::Float32;
  // 1797 digits, 64 pixels scaled to [0, 1] and centred (means over all 1797); the first 400 train, the last 360 test.
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::int64_t n = 1797, d = 64, n_train = 1437, n_fit = 400;
  std::vector<double> v(static_cast<std::size_t>(n * d));
  for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < d; ++j) {
    const auto c = digits.get_column<double>(std::format("pixel_{}", j)).values();
    const double m = std::accumulate(c.begin(), c.end(), 0.0) / n / 16.0;
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) v[i * d + j] = c[i] / 16.0 - m;
  }
  const auto label = digits.get_column<double>("digit").values();
  const Tensor X = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(v, {n, d}), F32);
  const Tensor y = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(label, {n}), nn::DType::Int64);
  const Tensor Xtr = nn::slice(X, {0, 0}, {n_fit, d}), Xte = nn::slice(X, {n_train, 0}, {n - n_train, d});
  const Tensor ytr = nn::slice(y, {0}, {n_fit}), yte = nn::slice(y, {n_train}, {n - n_train});

  // Ten hidden layers of width 64, no biases, each initialised with variance 1 / (64 E f(Z)^2) so that q = 1 is
  // the mean-field fixed point; full-batch Adam, learning rate 0.003, 60 steps, two seeds, Float32.
  // "swiglu" is a hidden layer 64 -> 128 followed by glu(., Silu): a * silu(b), back to width 64.
  // "+ res" variants use pre-norm residual blocks, h <- h + f(norm(h) W), with norm(h) = h / RMS(h) per row and
  // LeCun weights (variance 1/64) in the branch, as in a transformer feed-forward block.
  const int hidden = 10, steps = 60;
  const std::vector<std::uint64_t> seeds{1, 2};
  struct Run { std::string name; Activation act; bool gated; bool residual{false}; std::vector<double> loss; std::vector<double> acc; double scale{1}; };
  std::vector<Run> runs;
  for (Activation a : {Activation::Relu, Activation::LeakyRelu, Activation::Elu, Activation::Selu, Activation::Gelu, Activation::Silu,
                       Activation::Mish, Activation::HardSwish, Activation::Tanh, Activation::Softsign, Activation::Sigmoid})
    runs.push_back({std::string(nn::activation_name(a)), a, false});
  runs.push_back({"swiglu", Activation::Silu, true});
  runs.push_back({"relu + res", Activation::Relu, false, true});
  runs.push_back({"swiglu + res", Activation::Silu, true, true});
  for (auto& run : runs) {
    // Gated: E[(a silu(b))^2] = E a^2 E silu(b)^2 = 1 * E silu(Z)^2 at q = 1.
    run.scale = run.residual ? 1.0 : 1 / nn::activation_moments(run.act).second_moment;
    run.loss.assign(steps, 0.0);
    for (auto seed : seeds) {
      nn::ParameterStore store(seed, F32);
      std::vector<Tensor> W;
      for (int l = 0; l < hidden; ++l)
        W.push_back(store.get(std::format("W{}", l), {d, run.gated ? 2 * d : d}, init::variance_scaling(run.scale, init::FanMode::FanIn, init::Distribution::Normal)));
      W.push_back(store.get("out", {d, 10}, init::variance_scaling(1.0, init::FanMode::FanIn, init::Distribution::Normal)));
      nn::Optimizer opt(store, 0.003, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Adam});
      auto forward = [&](const Tensor& x) {
        Tensor h = x;
        for (int l = 0; l < hidden; ++l) {
          const Tensor in = run.residual ? h / nn::sqrt(nn::mean(nn::square(h), {-1}, true) + 1e-6) : h;
          const Tensor z = nn::matmul(in, W[l]);
          const Tensor f = run.gated ? nn::glu(z, Activation::Silu) : nn::activate(z, run.act);
          h = run.residual ? h + f : f;
        }
        return nn::matmul(h, W[hidden]);
      };
      for (int s = 0; s < steps; ++s) {
        const Tensor loss = nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(forward(Xtr), ytr);
        opt.step(nn::grad({loss}, W));
        run.loss[s] += loss.item() / seeds.size();
      }
      nn::NoGradGuard no_grad;
      run.acc.push_back(nn::mean(nn::cast(nn::equal(nn::argmax(forward(Xte), -1), yte), F32)).item());
    }
  }
  std::print("Mean square of a centred input pixel: {:.4f}.\n\n", nn::mean(nn::square(Xtr)).item());
  std::print("| activation | weight variance × 64 | train loss, step 1 | step 20 | step 40 | step 60 | test accuracy (mean of 2 seeds) | min–max over seeds |\n");
  std::print("| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& r : runs) {
    const double mean = std::accumulate(r.acc.begin(), r.acc.end(), 0.0) / r.acc.size();
    std::print("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.1f}% | {:.1f}–{:.1f}% |\n", r.name, r.scale, r.loss[0], r.loss[19], r.loss[39],
               r.loss.back(), 100 * mean, 100 * std::ranges::min(r.acc), 100 * std::ranges::max(r.acc));
  }

  // Left: loss curves for five activations; right: test accuracy, every seed, sorted.
  auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, dm::graphics::RGB>> shown = {
      {"relu", {37, 99, 235}}, {"selu", {220, 38, 38}}, {"tanh", {234, 88, 12}}, {"sigmoid", {148, 163, 184}}, {"swiglu", {124, 58, 237}}, {"swiglu + res", {22, 163, 74}}};
  std::vector<double> step;
  for (int s = 1; s <= steps; ++s) step.push_back(s);
  for (const auto& [name, col] : shown)
    for (const auto& r : runs)
      if (r.name == name) {
        std::vector<double> l;
        for (double x : r.loss) l.push_back(std::log10(x));
        pl.lines(step, l, name, col, 2.0);
      }
  pl.x_limits(0, steps).y_limits(-5.5, 4).title("Training loss (mean of 2 seeds)").x_label("Adam step").y_label("log10 cross-entropy");
  std::vector<std::size_t> order(runs.size());
  std::iota(order.begin(), order.end(), 0);
  std::ranges::sort(order, [&](auto a, auto b) { return std::accumulate(runs[a].acc.begin(), runs[a].acc.end(), 0.0) < std::accumulate(runs[b].acc.begin(), runs[b].acc.end(), 0.0); });
  auto pa = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<std::string> names;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < order.size(); ++i) {
    const auto& r = runs[order[i]];
    const double lo = 100 * std::ranges::min(r.acc), hi = 100 * std::ranges::max(r.acc);
    if (lo < 70) {  // off the axis: report the range in the label instead
      names.push_back(std::format("{} {:.0f}–{:.0f}%", r.name, lo, hi));
      continue;
    }
    names.push_back(r.name);
    std::vector<double> xs, ys;
    for (double a : r.acc) xs.push_back(100 * a), ys.push_back(double(i));
    pa.lines({100 * std::ranges::min(r.acc), 100 * std::ranges::max(r.acc)}, {double(i), double(i)}, dm::graphics::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.5);
    pa.points(xs, ys, dm::graphics::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 4.0);
  }
  pa.y_tick_labels(names).y_limits(-0.7, runs.size() - 0.3).x_limits(70, 95).title("Test accuracy, each seed").x_label("accuracy on 360 held-out digits (%)").y_label("");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(pl).add(pa);
  fig.size(1100, 460).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/activations_digits.svg");
}
```
