\image(figures/activations_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: training cross-entropy on a log10 scale, averaged over two seeds, for six of the fourteen networks. Right: test accuracy of each seed for every network, sorted by the mean. Sigmoid and the plain SwiGLU stack are off the scale, and their ranges are given in the labels.)
\alttext(Left: the purple plain-SwiGLU line is flat at about 0.36, the chance level, for all 60 steps; the grey sigmoid line lies under it until about step 40 and then dips only slightly, to 0.3. ReLU, SELU, tanh and residual SwiGLU fall from 0.4. Residual SwiGLU falls fastest, reaching minus 2 by step 10 and minus 3.9 by step 60. SELU reaches about minus 3.8 and ReLU, after a slower start, about minus 5 by step 60; tanh levels off near minus 2.2. Right: from the top, ReLU with residual blocks, SELU, tanh, ELU, softsign, hard swish, SiLU, GELU, leaky ReLU, ReLU and Mish have points between about 78 and 89.5 per cent, with seed ranges of up to 9 points. Residual SwiGLU's points lie at 73.3 and 74.4 per cent. The labels for sigmoid, 18 to 19 per cent, and plain SwiGLU, 10 per cent, have no points.)

The input's mean square is 0.0735 on the 400 training rows (0.0733 over all 1437 rows of the usual training split, the value used for the gated map above), so a plain stack starts at \(q_1=0.0735\,s\) rather than at its fixed point. For an ordinary activation that is harmless. Every plain network except sigmoid and SwiGLU fits the training set,
with loss of at most 0.0006 by step 60, or \(5.9\times10^{-3}\) and \(1.6\times10^{-2}\) for tanh and softsign. All of them reach 81.7% to 85.3% mean test accuracy, lower than a full training split would give. With seed ranges of up to 9 points
(leaky ReLU, 78.1% to 87.2%; softsign, 78.1% to 87.5%), two seeds cannot rank them. Once each is initialised at its fixed point, the choice among rectifiers matters little here.

The two failures are the ones the moments predict. Sigmoid, with \(\chi^\ast=0.15\), loses about 8 orders of magnitude of gradient variance over ten layers. Its loss is still 2.30, chance level, at step 40 and only 2.00 at step 60, and its
test accuracy is 18% to 19%. The plain SwiGLU stack starts at \(q_1\approx0.206\) on the repelling side of its fixed point. Its signal collapses, the output is essentially zero, and the loss equals \(\ln10=2.3026\) at every
checkpoint, which Adam cannot escape because the gradients collapse with the signal. Its test accuracy, 9.7%, is chance. The same units in pre-norm residual blocks fit the training set fastest of all
(loss 0.0011 at step 20). The normalisation resets \(q\) to 1 at every block, and the residual path carries the signal around the unstable map. They reach 73.9% on the test set, the lowest of the
networks that trained, with twice as many weights per layer, few training rows and no regularisation. ReLU in the same blocks reaches 88.2%, the highest mean.

> Conclusion

The shape of an activation is less important than its Gaussian moments. \(\mathbb E[f(Z)^2]\) fixes the weight scale at which the forward signal neither vanishes nor explodes. At that scale,
\(\chi^\ast=\mathbb E[f'^2]/\mathbb E[f^2]\) decides the gradients: exactly 1 for the rectifiers, slightly above 1 for the smooth rectifiers and SELU, and far below 1 for the sigmoid family, whose
non-zero mean wastes the signal's variance. SELU's constants follow in closed form from two moment equations, to 15 significant digits, and its fixed point attracts, but its \(\chi^\ast=1.07\) still lets gradients grow
by 7% per layer. The mean field predicts 30-layer networks to within 6%.

Gated units break the pattern. Their mean-field map is superlinear, so the fixed point repels and a plain stack collapses or explodes within a few layers. That is why SwiGLU is used inside
normalised residual blocks. On a 400-digit training set, every activation with \(\chi^\ast\) near 1 trained to 82% to 85% held-out accuracy once initialised at its own scale, sigmoid stalled, and plain SwiGLU never left chance.
In pre-norm residual blocks SwiGLU trained fastest of all.

